their hydrophilicity/hydrophobicity and the formation of Si–O–Si bonds on the surface, thereby affecting the friction and wear of the material in the tribological reaction [14,15]. Much research has been done on the effect of surface chemical species (i.e., Si–H, Si–OH, and Si–O–Si) on silicon-based materials on their tribological behavior in aqueous environments [9,14–24]. Based on these investigations, it is widely acknowledged that water molecules can be adsorbed onto silicon surfaces. Some of these water molecules undergo dissociation, resulting in the formation of H atoms and OH groups, a phenomenon known as dissociative adsorption. Conversely, certain adsorbed water molecules remain intact without dissociation, a process referred to as molecular adsorption. The surface of the silicon-containing material was terminated with H2O, H, and OH species. The silicon surface terminates with H species, as Si–H is hydrophobic, and is hydrophilic [25] when it is covered with Si–OH groups. Hydrophobic silicon surfaces exhibit high friction and wear [9], which is beneficial for the mechanical removal of material. The procedure of dehydration and dehydrogenation reactions occurring between the Si–OH groups on the surface and the adjoining Si atoms can lead to the formation of cohesive Si–O–Si bonds at the interface [20,21]. These previous studies suggest that the formation of interfacial Si–O–Si bonds plays a key role in the surface wear of Si. Nevertheless, various other simulations conducted on the subject of friction have revealed that the migration of H atoms across the contact interface and into the subsurface region [26,27], as well as the incorporation of O atoms into the friction surface through the formation of OH groups [28], can also result in bond rupture and degradation of the material. Unlike silicon-based materials, SiC is much more chemically inert, and chemical reactions are more difficult to take place on SiC surfaces [5,8,29,30]. Many studies have been conducted on the friction and wear of the SiC surface [4,29–38], and the formation of interfacial Si–O–Si bonds was also found to play an important role in the surface wear of SiC materials. However, these studies mainly focus on the mechanical wear of SiC and the thermal oxidation mechanism of SiC materials. So far, few studies have been made on the effect of chemical functional groups on the surface of SiC materials on the oxidation of SiC surfaces, especially in non-aqueous solution systems. Methanol and ethanol solvents have been revealed in the previous report to have improved material removal rate (MRR) and significantly better surface quality than aqueous solvents in a fixed abrasive polishing of SiC [39]. In the present investigation, molecular dynamics simulations utilizing the ReaxFF method have been employed to examine the
dynamic reaction mechanism between the solvent and the 6H–SiC (001) surface at various temperatures. The surface of SiC (001) was exposed to three solvents, namely water, methanol, and ethanol, at temperatures of 300 K, 1000 K, and 2000 K to investigate the interfacial physical and chemical actions of solvent molecules on the exposed SiC (001) surface at varied temperatures. Due to its adeptness in modeling bond formation and dissociation, as well as simulating reactive systems with a significant atom count [40], the ReaxFF reactive force field proves to be a fitting method for examining the adsorption and dissociation mechanisms of solvent molecules on 6H–SiC surfaces. This assertion is supported by robust research in the field [34]. To validate the simulation results, polishing experiments, AFM measurement of surface roughness, and XPS surface composition analysis after solvent corrosion of SiC were conducted. The interaction mechanism between water and alcohol solvents on the surface of SiC was proposed based on experimental and simulation results to further our understanding of the chemical nature of polishing slurries on the SiC surface and to provide guidance on developing an effective and more economically viable polishing system for SiC wafer manufacturing.
2. Simulation and experimental methods
2.1. ReaxFF reactive force field method
This study employs molecular dynamics simulations utilizing the ReaxFF reaction force field to examine the reaction mechanism between the SiC crystal surface and the solvent. For the purpose of this investigation, the ReaxFF reactive force field and three-dimensional periodic boundary conditions were employed in X, Y, and Z directions to conduct molecular dynamics simulations in order to explore the reaction mechanisms occurring between the SiC crystal surface and solvent. Through the utilization of this multiscale simulation method, which effectively combines the MD method and the finite element method, an atomic description of the interface and a continuous description in the bulk was achieved, thereby enhancing the accessible length scales [41]. The potential function served as a key tool to describe molecular interactions, with the interatomic forces being derivable from its derivatives. Notably, the molecular dynamics (MD) modeling approach was highly suitable for exploring various aspects such as nanoscale cutting, interface interactions, crystal orientations, and cutting directions [42]. This was achieved to bridge the gap that exists between MD simulations and the physical micro/nano polishing processes while
