2.2. Computational setup
To reveal the reaction process between the 6H–SiC surface and solvents such as H2O, CH3OH, and C2H5OH, we established a model system comprising a SiC (001) substrate and a solution of H2O (or CH3OH, C2H5OH) to simulate the aforementioned interaction. In order to achieve a sensible density, we undergo a relaxation process within the NPT ensemble. Within a relaxation period, time of approximately 50 Ps, the system’s volume can attain a state of relative stability. The model buildup and calculation process is conducted as follows: the 6H–SiC unit cell consisting of 1600 Si atoms and 1600 C atoms is established through the software database, and the 001 crystal plane is exposed to form the SiC substrate orientation. Solvent molecules were added to the model cells, using the NPT ensemble with a pressure set at 1 atm. After undergoing equilibration at a temperature of 50 K for a period of 50 Ps, with the aid of a Berendsen [46] thermal bath possessing a damping constant of 10 fs, the system is brought to a minimum potential energy, thereby forming the ultimate model, illustrated in Fig. 1. The intricacies of these models are clearly outlined in Table 1. (t is the thickness of the SiC substrate, α represents the angle between the X-axis and the Z-axis in the unit coordinate system, while β denotes the angle between the Y-axis and the Z-axis in the same coordinate system. γ, on the other hand, signifies the angle between the X-axis and the Y-axis. N1 represents the overall quantity of solvent molecules in system. N (C) denotes the quantity of 1 monolayer (ML) carbon atoms, while N (Si) stands for the quantity of 1 monolayer (ML) silicon atoms.) Table 1 illustrates the prearranged dimensions (X, Y, Z) of the cell, the SiC substrate’s thickness (t), the count of Si atoms, and the number of solvent molecules. Then the applied three-dimensional periodic boundary conditions were set along the X, Y, and Z directions, the number of simulation steps at 2,000,000 and the time step to 0.25 fs, NVT ensemble, and SiC (Si/C/O/H/N/S) force field were employed to carry out the simulation process. The temperature is regulated by a Berendsen thermostat possessing a damping coefficient of 5 fs, and the overall duration of the simulation is 500 Ps. All simulation processes
were completed using the AMSjobs [40,47,48] calculation software ReaxFF module, the software version number is AMSjobs 2021.106. The influence of temperature on reaction kinetics implies that maintaining the desired kinetic behavior at the initial temperature can be achieved by increasing the temperature of the reaction system. To clearly illustrate the disparity in reaction rates between the sic surface and various solvents, three distinct temperatures were chosen to manifest the contrasting chemical reactivity of solvents on the sic surface. To study the effect of temperature, three temperature conditions, 300 K, 1000 K, and 2000 K, were adopted.
2.3. Materials and polishing experimental methods
In the polishing experiment, a colloidal silica polishing slurry with a particle size of 120 nm and a concentration of 20 wt% (Shanghai Yingzhi Grinding Co., Ltd.) was used to pre-polish the C side and Si face of the 2inch SiC single wafer (Jiangyin Lanke Crystal Materials Co., Ltd., China) at 0.8 psi pressure for 30 min to make the wafer surface state consistent. Deionized water, methanol (CH3OH, CAS no.67-56-1, ≥99.5 %), and ethanol (C2H5OH, CAS no.64-17-5, ≥99.7 %) were prayed onto the fixed abrasive pad to polish the C-side and Si-side of the SiC wafer, respectively. The CMP polishing investigations were executed upon an ultraprecise annular polishing apparatus (ZDHP-30, Zhibang, China) utilizing a hydrophilic self-sharpening adhesive abrasion surface measuring 300 mm in diameter, which had been furnished by Nanjing University of Aeronautics and Astronautics. Each chemical-mechanical planarization (CMP) experiment was conducted at an applied pressure of 17.2 Kpa for a duration of 10 min, with the solvent flow rate set at 20 mL per minute, and the speed of the platen and carrier set at 20 and 50 revolutions per minute, respectively (Table 2). Each CMP experiment was replicated three times. Each CMP experiment was repeated 3 times. The experiment of solvothermal corrosion of SiC was carried out in a 100 ml capacity hydrothermal vessel. SiC particles were placed in three vessels filled with 30 ml of deionized water, CH3OH, and C2H5OH solvents respectively. The filled vessels were sealed and placed in an oven at 120 ◦C for 120 h. The reaction products were analyzed for XPS analysis. The SiC wafer masses pre- and post-polishing were assessed utilizing an OHAUS precision analytical balance, boasting a degree of accuracy to within 0.01 mg. Subsequent to this, MRR values were computed via appropriate methodologies [49].:
