3.2. Surface reaction mechanisms of H2O, CH3OH, C2H5OH on SiC substrates
3.2.1. Surface reaction of H2O on SiC substrates
Fig. 4 graphically depicts the detailed surface reaction process of SiC with H2O solvent, where the reacting molecules are marked and highlighted to clearly track the evolution of reaction steps. The process starts with a H2O (marked as O1) molecule diffusing close to the SiC surface (Fig. 4(a)), followed by breaking the H–OH bond to form a Si–OH species with surface Si (marked as Si1) (Fig. 4(b)) and Si–H with another neighboring Si (marked as Si2). The Si (marked as Si4) adjacent to the formed Si–OH (marked as Si3, O3) may become chemically more reactive, and initiate strong chemical adsorption with an H2O molecule (marked as O2) from the liquid phase. The bond strength of one OH group in chemical-sorbed Si–HO–H is weakened, and bridges with neighboring Si–OH to form HO–Si–OH–Si intermediate as depicted in Fig. 4(d), this meta-stable intermediate is then deprotonated and transferred at the interface through the water phase Fig. 4(e), resulting in that Si–OH2 turns into Si–OH, and the adjacent Si–OH becoming Si–OH2, of the OH2 that eventually leaves the adjacent Si (Fig. 4(f)), a
switching identity of two adjacent Si3 and Si4 atoms in presence of liquid water. In addition, the newly formed Si–OH is also found to be capable of hopping between two neighboring Si as illustrated in Fig. 4(g)–(i)). The OH group bonded with one Si (marked as Si5) atom first approaches the adjacent Si6 atom to form Si–OH–Si, and then transfers to the adjacent Si atom via the Si–OH–Si intermediate (Fig. 4(g)–(i)), completing a cycle of OH group hopping.
3.2.2. Surface reaction of alcohols on SiC substrates
The reaction process of CH3OH and C2H5OH on SiC surfaces are similar, therefore only the reaction path of CH3OH solvent on SiC is presented in Fig. 5. For a better visual illustration, CH3OH molecules are marked and highlighted in these processes. Different from the adsorption and dissociation of H2O on the surface of SiC, there are two reaction pathways for CH3OH on the SiC surface. One is that when CH3OH diffuses towards the SiC surface, the O atom (marked as O1) in CH3OH is selectively adsorbed on a Si (marked as Si1) atom (Fig. 5(a)) and then decomposes on the Si face of SiC to form Si–OH and an organosilicon species Si–CH3 (Fig. 5(b)), similarly to what happened with H2O. The other pathway is that when CH3OH approaches the Si face of SiC, its O atom of the CH3OH directly bonds to the surface Si to form Si–OHCH3. The adjacent Si–OH captures the H in Si–OHCH3 intermediates to generate Si–H2O and Si–OCH3, and the H2O molecule of the formed Si–H2O later desorbs and leaves from the Si face (Fig. 5(e)).
