3.3. Oxidation reaction mechanisms of solvents on SiC surface
3.3.1. Oxidation of SiC surface by H2O
The chemical reaction of SiC with solvent molecules is investigated by tracking the reaction steps of the marked Si, C, and H2O molecules as depicted in Fig. 8. After the initial stage of reactions, the detailed reaction process is shown in Fig. 8(a-i). First, 3H2O molecules react with surface SiC to form 3 Si–OH and 3 C–H as depicted in Fig. 8(a), and each Si–OH is surrounded to three C–H, so is each C–H to three Si–OH. One of the newly formed C–H leads to Si(1)-C bond breaking, loosing a C–H in the process (Fig. 2(b)). The Si(1)-OH that breaks bonding with C–H is activated thereafter, and bonds with neighboring Si(3)-OH to form OH–Si(1)–OH–Si(3) surface species (Fig. 2(c)). Si(1) follows to break its other Si–C bond, and bonds with another neighboring Si–OH surface species to form HC-Si(1)=(OH)2–Si (Fig. 8(d)). In Fig. 8(e), Si(1) breaks down its last Si–C, to form three coordinated silicon oxy-oxide species. One by one, the three OH groups in surface Si(1) (OH)3 loose their hydrogen to adjacent Si–OH to form Si–HOH and Si(1)-O-Si (Fig. 8(f-i)). The H2O desorbs from the SiC surface to restore the surface Si to its initial unreacted state, and leaves the SiC surface partially covered with Si(1)-O-Si to complete the SiC surface oxidation process. In a brief summary, H2O first reacts with the SiC surface to form surface Si–OH and C–H. Of the formed C–H, OH–Si–C bond is being activated and weakened, and eventually is broken to form Si–OH, leading to C–Si(OH)3 formation. The formed Si-(OH)3 facilitates the last Si–C bond to be broken. All three H in Si–OH are readily taken away one by one by its adjacent Si-OHs to form Si–O–Si and Si–HOH. The later Si–HOH looses its H2O, and Si restores to its initial state.
