3.4.1. MRR, sa and surface morphology of polished SiC wafers
SiC substrates were polished by deionized water, methanol and ethanol solvents on a fixed abrasive pad. The MRRs of SiC substrates polished are compared in Fig. 11 along with the Sa results. Under the same polishing condition, polishing solvents apply a great influence on the MRR of SiC polishing [39], and exhibit different polishing performance toward to the C face and the Si face of SiC substrates [52]. In polishing experiments carried in this research on the C face (Fig. 11(a)), C2H5OH leads to the highest MRR at 43.07 μm/h, followed by H2O at 41.96 μm/h and CH3OH at 31.35 μm/h. On the Si face on the other hand (Fig. 11(b)), C2H5OH still provides the highest MRR at 82.99 μm/h, followed by CH3OH at 49.58 μm/h and H2O at 35.19 μm/h. Basically, no matter on C or Si face, C2H5OH displays the highest MRR among all three evaluated solvents, while CH3OH performs better on material removal of Si face and H2O polishes faster on C face of SiC, consistent with previous reports that C face is being polished through a reaction mechanism different from that of Si face. It becomes clear that on C face of SiC, aqueous solvent performs equally well as alcohol solvents. On the other hand, on Si face, alcohol solvents polish much more effectively
