4. Conclusions
In this paper, the reaction process of H2O, CH3OH, C2H5OH solvent molecules on SiC surface is studied by ReaxFF molecular dynamics simulation and polishing experiments. The simulation results show that the solvent molecule undergoes molecular adsorption and dissociative adsorption on the SiC surface, resulting in the surface covered with HO, OH, H, alkyl, alkoxy, and vinyl groups that bond to surface Si or C. The groups adsorbed on the surface of SiC can lead to the Si–C bond breaking. As the temperature rises, the surface reaction speeds up. The oxidation of SiC surface in water and alcohol solvent is divided into three stages: the first stage, the solvent molecules dissociate on the SiC surface and occupy the uncoordinated Si atoms and C atoms on the surface; the second stage, the solvent molecules dissociate the groups adsorbed on the surface of SiC, reduce the surface energy and promote the breaking of Si–C bonds; in the third stage, the migration of OH and H on the SiC surface induces the formation of Si–O–Si bonds, which promotes the continuous oxidation of the SiC surface. The results of solvent polishing experiment, atomic force microscope observation and solvent corrosion XPS experiment on SiC surface all support that the chemical activity of alcohols and water on the SiC surface differs significantly, especially on the Si-face of SiC, C2H5OH > CH3OH > H2O, where alcohols demonstrate higher chemical reactivity towards to SiC, validating results derived from ReaxFF simulation. Through these studies, we reveal in detail the mechanism of non-aqueous solvent enhanced material removal rate, and provide options for the use of novel nonaqueous polishing slurries in the third-generation semiconductor of silicon carbide polishing process.
